S139 LRC14 TRIANGULAR / AFFINE-OPERATOR CARRIER
==============================================================================
[0] Assumption challenge / quotient declaration
  considered vertices:
    runners, gaps, affine words in a,b, dyadic depth profiles,
    triangular/product lanes, perfect-number indices, C27 shells,
    Kpq owners, cubic factor packets, logarithmic depth, and proof obligations.
  chosen quotient:
    proof carriers plus affine-word depth profiles; exact M/Farey data stays attached.
  preserved predicate:
    branch label, unit/composition order, pair-owner packet, and state-lift fit.
  destroyed predicate:
    exact safe-time geometry and row-specific witnesses.
  challenged assumption:
    the scalar equality or a raw triangular number can replace q or M(S).

[1] Log/quadratic equality audit
  x*(2x-1)=2*log2(x)+1-x is exactly equivalent to
  x^2 = 1/2 + log2(x).
  gap g(x)=x^2-1/2-log2(x) has its minimum at x=0.849321800288019.
  minimum gap = 0.456964333972033 > 0, so there is no positive real solution.
  sample gaps:
    x=0.50: g(x)= 0.750000000000
    x=0.75: g(x)= 0.477537499279
    x=1.00: g(x)= 0.500000000000
    x=2.00: g(x)= 2.500000000000
    x=4.00: g(x)= 13.500000000000
  readout: this is a curvature guardrail, not an identity.

[2] Cubic factor packet from the prompt
  C(x)=((x-1)/2)*(x/2)*(x-1/2) has roots 0, 1/2, 1.
  It is a signed three-wall packet, analogous to depth-3/Bonferroni labels.
  exact comparison with x^2-1/2:
    x=1/2 : C(x)=0        x^2-1/2-C(x)=-1/4
    x=3/4 : C(x)=-3/256   x^2-1/2-C(x)=19/256
    x=1   : C(x)=0        x^2-1/2-C(x)=1/2
    x=2   : C(x)=3/4      x^2-1/2-C(x)=11/4
    x=4   : C(x)=21/2     x^2-1/2-C(x)=5
  intersections with x^2-1/2 occur near 0.699650887534 and 5.336057118155.
  readout: useful only as a typed three-factor carrier; it is not log2(x).

[3] Triangular and perfect-number product lane
  x*(2x-1)=T_{2x-1}.
  If x=2^(r-1) and 2^r-1 is prime, this is the even perfect number
  2^(r-1)*(2^r-1).
    r= 2: x=    2  2^r-1=    3  T=       6  perfect
    r= 3: x=    4  2^r-1=    7  T=      28  perfect
    r= 4: x=    8  2^r-1=   15  T=     120  composite-index
    r= 5: x=   16  2^r-1=   31  T=     496  perfect
    r= 6: x=   32  2^r-1=   63  T=    2016  composite-index
    r= 7: x=   64  2^r-1=  127  T=    8128  perfect
    r= 8: x=  128  2^r-1=  255  T=   32640  composite-index
    r= 9: x=  256  2^r-1=  511  T=  130816  composite-index
    r=10: x=  512  2^r-1= 1023  T=  523776  composite-index
    r=11: x= 1024  2^r-1= 2047  T= 2096128  composite-index
    r=12: x= 2048  2^r-1= 4095  T= 8386560  composite-index
    r=13: x= 4096  2^r-1= 8191  T=33550336  perfect
  LRC14 product lane comparison on p/(14p-1):
      p   p*(2p-1)   p*(14p-1)   difference from 7*p*(2p-1)
      1          1           13            6
      2          6           54           12
      3         15          123           18
      4         28          220           24
      5         45          345           30
      6         66          498           36
      7         91          679           42
  readout: perfect/triangular arithmetic belongs to the product side channel,
  the same family as p*q, not to the binding denominator q.

[4] Affine operators a(x)=x/2 and b(x)=x+1
  left-to-right words produce x -> x/2^h + beta,
  beta=sum 2^{-future_halves_at_each_b}.
  noncommutator: ab(x)-ba(x)=1/2.
  word families:
    n=1 stair beta=     1/2 depths=[1]                    depth_sum=1   expected=T_1=1
    n=1 block beta=     1/2 depths=[1]                    depth_sum=1   expected=1^2=1
    n=1 tail  beta=       1 depths=[0]                    depth_sum=0   expected=0
    n=2 stair beta=     3/4 depths=[2, 1]                 depth_sum=3   expected=T_2=3
    n=2 block beta=     1/2 depths=[2, 2]                 depth_sum=4   expected=2^2=4
    n=2 tail  beta=       2 depths=[0, 0]                 depth_sum=0   expected=0
    n=3 stair beta=     7/8 depths=[3, 2, 1]              depth_sum=6   expected=T_3=6
    n=3 block beta=     3/8 depths=[3, 3, 3]              depth_sum=9   expected=3^2=9
    n=3 tail  beta=       3 depths=[0, 0, 0]              depth_sum=0   expected=0
    n=4 stair beta=   15/16 depths=[4, 3, 2, 1]           depth_sum=10  expected=T_4=10
    n=4 block beta=     1/4 depths=[4, 4, 4, 4]           depth_sum=16  expected=4^2=16
    n=4 tail  beta=       4 depths=[0, 0, 0, 0]           depth_sum=0   expected=0
    n=5 stair beta=   31/32 depths=[5, 4, 3, 2, 1]        depth_sum=15  expected=T_5=15
    n=5 block beta=    5/32 depths=[5, 5, 5, 5, 5]        depth_sum=25  expected=5^2=25
    n=5 tail  beta=       5 depths=[0, 0, 0, 0, 0]        depth_sum=0   expected=0
    n=6 stair beta=   63/64 depths=[6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1]     depth_sum=21  expected=T_6=21
    n=6 block beta=    3/32 depths=[6, 6, 6, 6, 6, 6]     depth_sum=36  expected=6^2=36
    n=6 tail  beta=       6 depths=[0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0]     depth_sum=0   expected=0
  readout: triangular numbers arise from the depth profile of a staircase
  composition, while squares arise from block order.  Same letter counts,
  different order labels.  That is exactly the anti-scalarization lesson.

